A||||6||Urchin||Legal question (fire insurance)||||1||
Z||000000||Urchin||08-30-2001||11:13 PM||||Here's the situation.  A while back My roommate burned our apartment down while I was at work(grease fire).  We were told by the landlord to come and collect any possessions (sp?) worth keeping and the rest she would dispose of.  We came in to collect our things.  During the time we were there, my roommate's mother's insurance company shows up and says "don't touch anything, we'll take care of it"  They said they would take our belongings to "winmar"  for cleaning and repair.  While our belongings sat awaiting cleaning the insurance company suddenly decided they weren't sure if we would be covered or not.  6 months went by and they finally decided they were not going to cover us.  By this time our belongings had been practically destroyed by mold and "winmar" claimed them to be garbage.  <BR>We originally were planning on paying for the cleaning and repairs of our possessions out of our own pocket, until the insurance company stepped in and took control, then backs out after our things have been ruined.  But to get to the point, and the question at hand.  "Winmar" issued a bill to my roommate's mother for $1800 dollars for the storage fee over the 6 months.  Which her insurance company has refused to pay.  A lot of bull as far as I am concerned.  Maybe we could have done things differently, but the fact is that this is how the situation has played out and I'm wondering if anyone has had similar experiences and if anything can be done about it.<BR>Thanks...<P>(Sorry for the length) <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Urchin||08-31-2001||06:31 PM||||bumpedy bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||08-31-2001||07:03 PM||||Actually a pretty complex sounding set of issues. I'm assuming you had renters insurance and not structural insurance? You just had the contents insured and are only contesting the disposition of these items, right? Definitely worthy of using an attorney, as long as the value is at least $5,000. The first issue I see is whether the insurance company is obligated to pay, or if a court would obligate them to pay. The second issue is if the insurance companies aren't obligated, then are they responsible for the mismanagement of your goods? Did they automatically assume indemnification upon transference of your possessions to the other party? <P>I don't have any knowledge in this area, but it sounds as if it's worth pursuing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||08-31-2001||07:31 PM||||File a big lawsuit against your insurance company, accept their out-of-court settlement.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Urchin:<BR><B>Here's the situation.  A while back My roommate burned our apartment down while I was at work(grease fire).  We were told by the landlord to come and collect any possessions (sp?) worth keeping and the rest she would dispose of.  We came in to collect our things.  During the time we were there, my roommate's mother's insurance company shows up and says "don't touch anything, we'll take care of it"  They said they would take our belongings to "winmar"  for cleaning and repair.  While our belongings sat awaiting cleaning the insurance company suddenly decided they weren't sure if we would be covered or not.  6 months went by and they finally decided they were not going to cover us.  By this time our belongings had been practically destroyed by mold and "winmar" claimed them to be garbage.  <BR>We originally were planning on paying for the cleaning and repairs of our possessions out of our own pocket, until the insurance company stepped in and took control, then backs out after our things have been ruined.  But to get to the point, and the question at hand.  "Winmar" issued a bill to my roommate's mother for $1800 dollars for the storage fee over the 6 months.  Which her insurance company has refused to pay.  A lot of bull as far as I am concerned.  Maybe we could have done things differently, but the fact is that this is how the situation has played out and I'm wondering if anyone has had similar experiences and if anything can be done about it.<BR>Thanks...<P>(Sorry for the length) <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||Urchin||08-31-2001||07:45 PM||||My roommate and I did not have content insurance between the two of us (our mistake).  My roommates's mother's (we'll call her "Kathy" to simplify)insurance company stepped in and brought to our attention that since my roommate is currently a student that she would be covered under her mother's insurance (not sure how that works).  As I said, they stepped in and removed all items salvageable to be repaired or cleaned, thus deaming them responsible for the well being of our possessions (in my opinion).  Since this claim was through "Kathy's" insurance company I have no knowledge of the details of her policy, nor her interactions with the brokers during the period in question.  If they were not intending (or unsure if they would be) covering our belongings then they should not have jumped the gun and claimed they would "take care of everything".  Unfortunately my knowledge in this area is limited, hence my post.  This situation has now resulted in all possessions being destroyed (including those that could have been saved) and a $1800 storage fee to boot.  The fact is that if the insurance company had not stepped in, our belongings would have been saved.  We all pay monthly insurance fees for various belongings.  Our homes, our vehicles, prized possessions, etc.  When it comes time to make a claim, it seems they will find anyway possible to weasle out of it.  How is this fair? They took our statements regarding the details of the incident (night of the fire).  They also took a list of belongings that had been in the home at the time of the fire.  I don't know what papers "Kathy" may or may not have signed, as I am unsure of the process.  Hopefully this helps clarify things a little.  A very frusterating situation, which hopefully will end on a positive note.  Of course, I have my doubts.<BR>Thanks for the responce Ed||||reg||1
Z||000005||Urchin||08-31-2001||07:46 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>File a big lawsuit against your insurance company, accept their out-of-court settlement.<P><BR> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That just may be what it comes to   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 08-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||09-01-2001||03:04 PM||||Hey Urchin!<BR>Don't know Canadian law, but, when the insurance agent "took posession" of your property, they had the responsabillity of their care...<BR>As they gave the property to a cleaning company, the cleaning company also had responsabillity for the care of the property...<BR>So you should sue both parties for improper handling of your property...<BR>Depending on the value though, it might not be worth pusuing, but if it is, go for it!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
